n survive where the media people  don't like  we cannot survive with a media people do  not trust  when i was in congress there were some  house democrats who treated me and other  republicans with more fundamental  fairness than the d.c media did  and at the top of that list was a  progressive democrat from the state of  hawaii  former member of congress former  presidential candidate  and current american soldier  tulsi gabbard tulsi aloha  aloha my friend it's good to see you  it's good to see you also now i'm gonna  i'm gonna ask you  a tough question i perceive  that you were treated unfairly i  perceived that there were times in your  congressional career and as a  presidential  candidate that there was a different set  of rules applied to you was that my own  bias of liking you  or have you perceived that if you're a  little bit out of the democrat  mainstream maybe they don't treat you  the same  your perception would be accurate sir  i am not the only one who has been  subjected to  this bias that we've seen across the  media and unfortunately  uh i think none of this would come as a  surprise  to the american people who are who are  watching this show right now and it's  led to this distrust  in a media that should be presenting  itself  as true journalists with with unbiased  and neutral information  from which people who are watching can  can draw their own conclusions and  the reason for this is we've got a media  frankly that's driven  by profits in how do we get the highest  ratings  how do we get the most clicks in media  and  especially when you've got newsrooms  that have been moved into the  entertainment  divisions of these massive media  corporations  the news becomes entertainment and and  what is entertaining well drama and  conflict sells so  let's see and i saw this through through  the debates in the presidential campaign  especially but there's so many examples  of  let's see how we can fuel this conflict  culture and and divisiveness  to 